\section{Composition Continues After Parsing}

Two language modules can share a tree without sharing a meaning for names. A workflow language may export a task named \texttt{charge}; a data language may export a field with the same spelling. A reference in a workflow command and a reference in an expression should not simply retrieve whichever symbol was registered last. Define each reference site by its scope, spelling, and required symbol kind. Also define which imports or enclosing scopes are consulted and which outcomes count as ambiguity. The kind describes the role of the symbol in resolution; it is not necessarily the type of the value obtained by referring to it.

Arvid Butting, Judith Michael, and Bernhard Rumpe describe generated, strongly kind-typed interfaces for symbols and scopes in \textit{Language Composition via Kind-Typed Symbol Tables}. In MontiCore, handwritten adapters can present a symbol of one language as a symbol of a kind expected by another. Using the host language's type system for these interfaces prevents some integration mistakes; it cannot prove that the code inside an adapter gives the right meaning. Markus Voelter and collaborators, in \textit{DSL Engineering}, likewise put language composition beyond merely combining the concrete syntax.

\section{Worked Integration: A Field Used as an Expression}

Suppose a form module exports \texttt{Invoice.total}, a field whose declared type is \texttt{Money}. A checks module accepts \texttt{check Invoice.total > 0} and expects references in comparisons to resolve as readable values. The integration declares an adapter from visible, readable field symbols to readable-value symbols. The adapter exposes \texttt{Money} as the field's value type and provides a way to read the field. It does not expose field assignment, make private fields public, or change the identity of the defining declaration. Whether \texttt{0} is comparable with \texttt{Money} is still a question for the checker.

Resolve the qualifier \texttt{Invoice} under the module's import rules, select \texttt{total} among the visible exported candidates, and check its kind or applicable adaptation. If two visible exports of equal precedence both adapt to the required kind, reject the reference as ambiguous unless the language defines an explicit ordering. Conversely, a workflow task named \texttt{total} is not a value candidate merely because it shares the spelling. A stable binding record should retain the identity of the original field as well as the adapter used. That lets go-to-definition point to the field and gives rename tooling a way to find the cross-language reference.

Test a valid import, a private field, a task with the same spelling, and two equally visible candidates. Remove the adapter and confirm that the reference fails with a kind-specific diagnostic, not a parser error. If order is supposed to be irrelevant, repeat the tests with the modules registered in reverse order. In an incremental implementation, a successful lookup depends on the set of candidates considered, not just the declaration selected: a newly visible competitor can change success into ambiguity.

\section{Binding Introduced by Expansion}

Extensions can introduce names as well as look them up. Justin Pombrio and Shriram Krishnamurthi's \textit{Inferring Type Rules for Syntactic Sugar} shows why deriving a surface type rule from a desugaring can be preferable to reporting an error only in generated core code. Their example of short-circuit disjunction uses a temporary binding so that the left expression is not evaluated twice. This suggests two separate obligations for an expansion: its generated references must resolve to the intended binders, and its result must obey the type rule promised at the surface.

For a short-circuit expression \texttt{a or tmp}, expanding with a textual local also named \texttt{tmp} can capture the reference in the right operand. Give the generated binder a fresh identity and preserve the original operand's use-site binding; do not rely on a spelling convention that a user can happen to choose. Retain source origins for generated nodes so a checker can blame the right operand when it fails. The provenance interface in \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/06-bidirectional-compilers.md}{Markdown Dossier Chapter~06} explains why such a link is useful even when there is no inverse translation. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-139} treats binding preservation as a precondition for changing already written source.
